🎯 The Core Question Behind This Question

Both let you achieve Abstraction (remember that? 🎭). Both say "here's WHAT must be done, not necessarily HOW." So WHY do we need both?? That's the real thing interviewers are testing — do you understand WHEN each tool is the right one, not just their syntax differences.

🏗️ The Analogy: Building a House vs Following Building Codes

Abstract Class = A Half-Built House 🏚️

Imagine you inherited a half-finished house from your dad. The FOUNDATION, WALLS, and ROOF are already built (some concrete stuff done for you) — but the INTERIOR DESIGN is left for YOU to finish. You didn't start from zero, you got a HEAD START with some real, working structure already in place.

Interface = A Building CODE/Regulation 📜

A building code doesn't build ANYTHING for you. It just says: "Your house MUST have a fire exit, MUST have proper ventilation, MUST have electrical safety." It's a CONTRACT of requirements — you build EVERYTHING yourself, the code just dictates the RULES you must follow.

That's the fundamental difference: Abstract class gives you a partial implementation (some code already written). Interface gives you a pure contract (zero implementation, historically). 🔑

💻 Show Me The Code
Abstract Class:
java
abstract class Vehicle {
    String brand = "Generic"; // ✅ CAN have actual fields/state

    void start() {  // ✅ CAN have fully implemented (concrete) methods
        System.out.println(brand + " is starting... 🔑");
    }

    abstract void accelerate();  // ❌ MUST be implemented by child (no body)
}

class Car extends Vehicle {  // uses "extends" — only ONE allowed!
    void accelerate() {
        System.out.println("Car is speeding up 🚗💨");
    }
}
Interface:
java
interface Flyable {
    void fly();  // ❌ traditionally NO body, just a contract
}

interface Swimmable {
    void swim();  // ❌ same, just a contract
}

class Duck implements Flyable, Swimmable {  // uses "implements" — MULTIPLE allowed!
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Duck is flying 🦆✈️");
    }
    public void swim() {
        System.out.println("Duck is swimming 🦆🌊");
    }
}

Notice immediately: Car extends Vehicle (singular, ONE parent only) vs Duck implements Flyable, Swimmable (MULTIPLE interfaces at once!). This is honestly the BIGGEST practical difference and the one interviewers zero in on. 👀